WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.810
Every creator says the same thing: it came to me.

2
00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:10.340
But if it came, then it was already somewhere — and if it was already somewhere, what exactly did you make?

3
00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:17.030
Tonight we ask whether creativity is an act of bringing into being, or only the pleasure of being the first to find.

4
00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:30.640
Picture the moment you walk into the kitchen in the early evening and open the refrigerator door.

5
00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:37.650
Inside there is half an onion, two eggs, a sprig of parsley left over from yesterday, a bit of cheese at the back.

6
00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:42.900
Nobody chose these ingredients for you, and you did not buy them with tonight in mind.

7
00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:51.830
And yet fifteen minutes later there will be a meal on the table, and that meal will be something that has never existed in exactly that form anywhere in the world before.

8
00:00:51.830 --> 00:00:59.480
Now here is the real question: did you create that meal, or did you merely arrange things that were already in your hands into a new order?

9
00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:04.570
And if it is the second, then is there really such a separate activity as creating at all?

10
00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:11.580
At first glance this looks like a question about cooking, but it connects to one of the oldest veins in philosophy.

11
00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:18.110
Very early in Greek thought, the idea that nothing can come out of nothingness was accepted almost as a law of nature.

12
00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:25.120
Along the line that runs from Parmenides to Lucretius, the phrase was repeated plainly: nothing comes from nothing.

13
00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:28.290
Aristotle was looking from the same place.

14
00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:33.380
For him, every act of making was the giving of form to something that already existed.

15
00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:37.990
The carpenter does not create the wood; he gives the wood the shape of a table.

16
00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:42.920
The sculptor does not bring marble into being; he draws a face out of the marble.

17
00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:48.170
To make was to clothe material in a form, not to bring it out of nothing.

18
00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:53.580
The idea of creation out of nothing entered philosophy mostly through the door of theology.

19
00:01:53.580 --> 00:02:01.070
In all three of the Abrahamic traditions, the power to bring something into being out of nothing was held to belong to God alone.

20
00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:13.040
In Islamic thought this distinction is woven into the very fabric of the language: names such as Hâlik, Bâri' and Musavvir belong to God, while other words are used for the work a human being does.

21
00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:18.290
In classical Turkish, too, one does not say that a poet created a poem.

22
00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:23.700
A poem is nazmedilir, that is, it is strung, it is arranged in order.

23
00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:28.790
A person who writes a book is a müellif, and the work itself is a telif.

24
00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:36.760
The root of that word says a great deal: telif means to join, to warm one thing to another, to establish ülfet, familiarity.

25
00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:42.970
In other words, in the classical tradition the author is not someone who makes things out of nothing,

26
00:02:42.970 --> 00:02:49.500
but someone who brings together things that stand as strangers to one another and establishes a closeness between them.

27
00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:55.070
The strange thing is that Western languages say exactly the same thing, only more quietly.

28
00:02:55.070 --> 00:03:01.760
The word composition, used for a piece of music, means in Latin to place side by side, to put together.

29
00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:08.290
The Greek word poiesis, used for poetry, means straightforwardly to make, to manufacture.

30
00:03:08.290 --> 00:03:18.500
In Arabic, the word for poet comes from a root meaning to know and to sense; the poet is not someone who invents a thing, but someone who notices a thing others have failed to notice.

31
00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:28.070
For thousands of years, languages have been whispering the same message to us: the human task is to find, to choose, to arrange, to combine.

32
00:03:28.070 --> 00:03:31.400
So why does this idea bother us so much?

33
00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:35.050
Because we like believing that we made something from zero.

34
00:03:35.050 --> 00:03:45.100
Someone writing a song, an architect, a software developer, someone illustrating a children's book — each wants the thing that emerges to be theirs.

35
00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:52.110
And the sentence "actually, you only rearranged pieces that already existed" rings in the ear like an insult.

36
00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:54.960
As if it shrinks your labor.

37
00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:58.130
As if it takes away the value of what you have done.

38
00:03:58.130 --> 00:04:03.060
But that sentence does not shrink the labor; it moves where the labor sits.

39
00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:05.750
Let us go back to the refrigerator.

40
00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:10.680
The skill in that evening's meal did not lie in conjuring the ingredients out of nothing.

41
00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:17.050
The skill lay in looking at those four or five things and seeing a relationship among them that nobody else had seen.

42
00:04:17.050 --> 00:04:20.540
In knowing what the parsley would do with the cheese.

43
00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:27.870
In having learned, from fifteen years of ruined dinners, exactly how many seconds longer the egg needs on the heat.

44
00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:34.400
The ability, in other words, is hidden not in the raw material but in the relation and in the decision.

45
00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:38.370
And this is precisely where the tension we are going to pick at begins.

46
00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:43.140
If what we call creating is largely finding, then where does that finding come from?

47
00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:49.350
Some will call it a voice from outside: inspiration, the breath of the muse, genius.

48
00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:55.400
Others will say everything is inside, that the mind collides the pieces together in a silent room.

49
00:04:55.400 --> 00:05:02.410
And others will say something more uncomfortable still: perhaps we are not finding anything, perhaps we only think we are.

50
00:05:02.410 --> 00:05:11.500
Perhaps what we do amounts to a small shift of position inside a structure that has been under construction for hundreds of years, and we write our own name on that small shift.

51
00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:18.990
Humanity's first answer to this question was, surprisingly, full of humility: I did not make it, it was said through me.

52
00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:22.960
The oldest texts of Greek poetry begin with an invitation.

53
00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:29.490
Before the poet speaks, he stops and calls out to the goddesses: tell me, sing through my mouth.

54
00:05:29.490 --> 00:05:38.900
Hesiod, in the book where he recounts the birth of the gods, says that while he was herding sheep on the mountain the muses came to him and placed a branch of laurel in his hand.

55
00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:45.270
This is not a literary ornament but a serious claim: the source of the words is not the poet himself.

56
00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:49.400
Plato took that claim and carried it to its furthest point.

57
00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:56.410
In the dialogue called Ion, he openly refuses to count poetry as an art, that is, as a teachable technique.

58
00:05:56.410 --> 00:06:04.380
For him the poet is like an iron ring that has touched a magnet; his power comes not from himself but from the divine source at the end of the chain.

59
00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:11.390
While the poet speaks he is not himself, he is not in his right mind.

60
00:06:11.390 --> 00:06:22.240
And notice, it is still argued today whether this is praise or a subtle insult, because in the same breath Plato grants the poet a divine privilege and implies that he does not know what he is saying.

61
00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:27.490
In another dialogue he speaks of four kinds of sacred madness and counts poetry among them.

62
00:06:27.490 --> 00:06:31.940
Creativity, in other words, is a form of losing one's mind.

63
00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:39.910
When Islamic philosophy met the same question, it built a far more systematic answer, but the direction it pointed was still outward.

64
00:06:39.910 --> 00:06:45.640
In Fârâbî there is a layer of the human mind called the mütehayyile, the imaginative faculty.

65
00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:50.570
This power can break apart the material arriving from the senses and recombine it.

66
00:06:50.570 --> 00:06:59.980
But the critical point in Fârâbî's system is that once this faculty reaches a certain maturity, it receives an overflow from an intellect above it, from the Active Intellect.

67
00:06:59.980 --> 00:07:05.070
What is new, then, is not built from the bottom upward; it descends from above.

68
00:07:05.070 --> 00:07:10.000
Ibn Sînâ carried this idea to a much more personal place.

69
00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:14.450
In his thought there is a concept called hads: intuition.

70
00:07:14.450 --> 00:07:22.580
Some people arrive at a piece of knowledge by reasoning step by step, while others skip the intermediate rungs and reach the conclusion all at once.

71
00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:33.110
Ibn Sînâ says this capacity is distributed among people by degrees, that its highest degree is seen in prophecy, and he considers his own power of intuition to be strong.

72
00:07:33.110 --> 00:07:39.640
He describes waking one night while wrestling with a difficult problem and finding the solution opened out before him.

73
00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:49.370
The trouble is that structurally this account is not so different from Plato's chain of magnets: what is new is not something the human being builds, but something given to the human being.

74
00:07:49.370 --> 00:07:56.540
By the time Europe reached the eighteenth century, this outside voice had moved indoors, but it had not disappeared.

75
00:07:56.540 --> 00:08:08.030
The Latin origin of the word genius is a guardian spirit believed to accompany a person; at the beginning, in other words, the genius was a being beside the human, not the human himself.

76
00:08:08.030 --> 00:08:14.080
The Romantic period reversed this: now the spirit was not outside but inside the artist.

77
00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:22.690
In his work on aesthetics, published in seventeen ninety, Kant defines genius this way: genius is the natural talent that gives the rule to art.

78
00:08:22.690 --> 00:08:30.020
The person of genius does not apply a rule, he brings a rule into being, and he himself cannot explain how he did it.

79
00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:34.310
The goddess outside had turned into an inexplicable core inside.

80
00:08:34.310 --> 00:08:37.320
But the inexplicability remained the same.

81
00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:40.650
That inheritance is in all of our mouths today.

82
00:08:40.650 --> 00:08:43.980
When an idea arrives we say it came to me.

83
00:08:43.980 --> 00:08:47.470
It is something that comes; we do not make it, it arrives.

84
00:08:47.470 --> 00:08:53.520
It comes in the shower, it comes on the bus, it comes in the place between sleep and waking.

85
00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:56.210
And these accounts are not empty.

86
00:08:56.210 --> 00:09:07.060
The French mathematician Poincaré, in the essay where he described his own processes of discovery, says that the solution to a problem he had struggled with fruitlessly for days came to him suddenly,

87
00:09:07.060 --> 00:09:11.350
in the moment he set his foot on the step to board a carriage during a journey.

88
00:09:11.350 --> 00:09:19.960
The chemist Kekulé, in a talk he gave years afterward, tied the structure of the benzene molecule to the image of a snake biting its own tail.

89
00:09:19.960 --> 00:09:23.130
But look at those two accounts a little more carefully.

90
00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:27.740
Poincaré did find that solution as his foot touched the step, true.

91
00:09:27.740 --> 00:09:32.030
But he had been working on that problem for weeks beforehand.

92
00:09:32.030 --> 00:09:38.880
When Kekulé saw the snake he was a chemist who had spent decades dealing with the bond structures of molecules.

93
00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:44.130
If a farmer or a tailor had seen the same image, no benzene ring would have come out of it.

94
00:09:44.130 --> 00:09:49.700
The voice that arrives, in other words, always speaks the language you taught it.

95
00:09:49.700 --> 00:09:58.150
Edison's much repeated line says precisely this: genius is one percent inspiration and ninety-nine percent perspiration.

96
00:09:58.150 --> 00:10:03.560
That sentence is usually read as praise for hard work, but it says something sharper.

97
00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:08.330
That one percent really exists, and it really does arrive out of nowhere.

98
00:10:08.330 --> 00:10:12.300
But without the ninety-nine percent it has nowhere to arrive.

99
00:10:12.300 --> 00:10:20.430
Inspiration does not walk into an empty room; in a full room, among furniture carried in beforehand, it establishes an order.

100
00:10:20.430 --> 00:10:24.560
So the feeling of having found something is not an illusion.

101
00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:28.850
The illusion concerns where that finding came from.

102
00:10:28.850 --> 00:10:34.260
We tell ourselves a story: the idea came from outside, I merely opened my vessel.

103
00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:42.070
What actually happens is closer to this: the pieces we have been collecting for years touched one another while we were busy with something else.

104
00:10:42.070 --> 00:10:45.080
And where do those pieces come from?

105
00:10:45.080 --> 00:10:49.530
The answer is uncomfortable, because most of them come from other people.

106
00:10:49.530 --> 00:10:56.540
And this touches the itchiest spot in the modern mind: if my material belongs to others, how much of it is my share?

107
00:10:56.540 --> 00:11:00.830
Where does imitation end and where does what is mine begin?

108
00:11:00.830 --> 00:11:08.960
When we ask that question today, we ask it against a powerful background assumption: originality is good, imitation is bad.

109
00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:15.010
A student writing a paper hesitates to read too many sources, afraid of being influenced.

110
00:11:15.010 --> 00:11:19.620
A musician tries listening to nothing at all in order to find his own voice.

111
00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:24.390
An entrepreneur is crushed on learning that the idea has been tried before.

112
00:11:24.390 --> 00:11:31.080
These fears seem so natural to us that we fail to notice that for most of history people thought exactly the opposite.

113
00:11:31.080 --> 00:11:33.770
Look at the Ottoman poetic tradition.

114
00:11:33.770 --> 00:11:37.740
There you find an institutionalized practice called nazire.

115
00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:44.110
A poet takes the meter and rhyme scheme of a poem he admires and writes his own poem in that same mold.

116
00:11:44.110 --> 00:11:49.360
He does not hide this; on the contrary, he wants it known which poem he is answering.

117
00:11:49.360 --> 00:11:53.330
Because this is not copying, it is a challenge.

118
00:11:53.330 --> 00:11:58.260
It is a competition in which everyone uses the same instrument, tuned the same way.

119
00:11:58.260 --> 00:12:06.070
Mastery lies not in inventing a melody nobody has heard, but in playing the melody everybody knows in a way nobody has managed to play it.

120
00:12:06.070 --> 00:12:18.840
The same metaphors, the same rose and nightingale, the same moon-faced beloved circulate for hundreds of years, and the poet's success consists in finding a new turn inside that familiar material.

121
00:12:18.840 --> 00:12:24.570
A single line that came out of this tradition may have stated our real question more openly than anyone.

122
00:12:24.570 --> 00:12:37.180
Şeyh Galib, in the mesnevi he wrote in seventeen eighty-three, Hüsnü Aşk, says that he took the secrets of his work from Rumi's Mesnevî, and then immediately adds: I stole, but what I stole was public property.

123
00:12:37.180 --> 00:12:41.150
State property, the property of the commons.

124
00:12:41.150 --> 00:12:44.000
That sentence does two things at once.

125
00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:48.130
On one hand it openly acknowledges the debt and does not hide it.

126
00:12:48.130 --> 00:12:56.100
On the other it declares that the debt is nothing to be ashamed of, because what he took is nobody's private possession but the shared treasury of us all.

127
00:12:56.100 --> 00:13:07.910
And notice, Şeyh Galib is not saying this out of modesty; in that same work he is a poet of enormous ambition, twenty-six years old, complaining that those who came before him failed to say anything new.

128
00:13:07.910 --> 00:13:12.200
Leaning on tradition and being original are not opposites in his mind.

129
00:13:12.200 --> 00:13:21.930
Ibn Khaldun, in the Muqaddima he completed in thirteen seventy-seven, strips this matter of sentiment and turns it into something close to a sociological law.

130
00:13:21.930 --> 00:13:29.580
For him arts and crafts are each a meleke, that is, habits acquired through repetition that settle into the body and the mind.

131
00:13:29.580 --> 00:13:36.590
These habits are not born out of books or out of a person's own self-discovery; they are learned from a master.

132
00:13:36.590 --> 00:13:44.720
A craft can therefore develop only inside a settled city life that has stood for a long time and that contains masters able to teach it.

133
00:13:44.720 --> 00:13:52.690
The conclusion Ibn Khaldun draws from this is a hard one: creativity is not an individual spark but the product of an accumulation.

134
00:13:52.690 --> 00:13:56.500
A genius without a master is a theoretical assumption.

135
00:13:56.500 --> 00:14:02.550
The sequence that Mimar Sinan set out when he looked back on his own life carries the same logic.

136
00:14:02.550 --> 00:14:15.640
In the account attributed to him, the Şehzade Mosque in Istanbul is his apprentice work, the Süleymaniye his journeyman work, and the Selimiye in Edirne, completed in fifteen seventy-five, his masterwork.

137
00:14:15.640 --> 00:14:18.970
There is no zero point anywhere in that story.

138
00:14:18.970 --> 00:14:27.420
There is a man who spent years alongside other masters, on other building sites, learning how a dome holds itself up and where a stone will crack.

139
00:14:27.420 --> 00:14:36.030
The Selimiye is not something a genius pulled out of the void; it is the furthest point that a dome tradition of many centuries reached in one human being.

140
00:14:36.030 --> 00:14:42.720
But we have to see the other face of this as well, or a defense of tradition easily turns into conservatism.

141
00:14:42.720 --> 00:14:52.770
Yunus Emre, instead of using the heavy poetic language expected of him, loaded with Arabic and Persian constructions, spoke in the plain Turkish of ordinary people.

142
00:14:52.770 --> 00:14:58.180
He was inside the tradition; he knew the concepts of Sufism, its metaphors.

143
00:14:58.180 --> 00:15:03.750
What he did was not to escape the tradition but to carry the tradition's material into another language.

144
00:15:03.750 --> 00:15:10.120
Tradition, then, is not a prison, but neither is it a deposit to be carried onward unchanged.

145
00:15:10.120 --> 00:15:15.370
And the assumption that strict rules suffocate creativity is not as solid as we think either.

146
00:15:15.370 --> 00:15:23.020
The aruz meter, the sonnet, the haiku, even the character limit on a piece of advertising copy are all constraints.

147
00:15:23.020 --> 00:15:28.590
But that constraint sets in motion a mind that would otherwise freeze in front of infinite options.

148
00:15:28.590 --> 00:15:33.360
If everything were in the refrigerator, deciding what to make would take you longer.

149
00:15:33.360 --> 00:15:36.690
Four ingredients force you to find a solution.

150
00:15:36.690 --> 00:15:45.140
Children play this way too: the blanket, the chair and the cushion in their hands turn into a tent; the missing things generate the rules of the game.

151
00:15:45.140 --> 00:15:50.550
The tension between imitation and originality, then, is not really a choice.

152
00:15:50.550 --> 00:15:53.720
Nobody can speak without learning a language.

153
00:15:53.720 --> 00:15:59.130
But nobody can spend a lifetime speaking by repeating only the sentences he has heard.

154
00:15:59.130 --> 00:16:08.700
At this point a far simpler and far more powerful definition of creating appears: to create is to bring two things that have never touched into contact with one another.

155
00:16:08.700 --> 00:16:19.230
Arthur Koestler, in the book he published in nineteen sixty-four, The Act of Creation, gives this operation a name: joining two separate planes of thought at the same time.

156
00:16:19.230 --> 00:16:27.360
A joke works this way, and so does a scientific discovery; in both, two frames that stood apart suddenly lie one on top of the other.

157
00:16:27.360 --> 00:16:39.010
Claude Lévi-Strauss, in the book published in nineteen sixty-two, The Savage Mind, offers a different image: the craftsman who works with whatever scattered leftover material is at hand, the bricoleur.

158
00:16:39.010 --> 00:16:44.420
This craftsman does not order the part he needs; he thinks with whatever is in his box.

159
00:16:44.420 --> 00:16:49.510
And according to Lévi-Strauss, the human mind largely works like this.

160
00:16:49.510 --> 00:16:53.000
Look at history with this eye and the landscape changes.

161
00:16:53.000 --> 00:16:59.050
What Gutenberg did in the middle of the fifteenth century was not to invent a technology nobody knew.

162
00:16:59.050 --> 00:17:10.060
Europe had been using wine and olive presses for centuries, metal type and the punching of molds were known in goldsmithing and in the striking of coins, paper had long since arrived, ink existed.

163
00:17:10.060 --> 00:17:13.870
Gutenberg built the arrangement that held these pieces together.

164
00:17:13.870 --> 00:17:18.800
The pieces were lying in the open; he was the one who put them on the same table.

165
00:17:18.800 --> 00:17:24.850
The most striking evidence for this is the surprising frequency of simultaneous invention in history.

166
00:17:24.850 --> 00:17:34.260
Darwin had been working on the origin of species for years; in eighteen fifty-eight he received a letter from Wallace, who had independently arrived at a similar mechanism.

167
00:17:34.260 --> 00:17:42.070
Bell and Gray filed their patent applications for the telephone on the same day, the fourteenth of February, eighteen seventy-six.

168
00:17:42.070 --> 00:17:49.560
Newton and Leibniz developed the calculus separately from each other, and the dispute over who found it first went on for years afterward.

169
00:17:49.560 --> 00:17:56.890
This recurring pattern tells us something: when an idea's time has come, there is more than one mind in a position to find it.

170
00:17:56.890 --> 00:18:00.220
Because the pieces are in front of everyone.

171
00:18:00.220 --> 00:18:03.550
This may look as though it diminishes creativity.

172
00:18:03.550 --> 00:18:10.400
I think it does the opposite, because it takes the weight of the work away from a mystical gift and places it on human shoulders.

173
00:18:10.400 --> 00:18:14.850
When the pieces are held in common, what remains to you is choice.

174
00:18:14.850 --> 00:18:17.540
Choosing which question to ask.

175
00:18:17.540 --> 00:18:21.510
Deciding which two things are worth trying to bring together.

176
00:18:21.510 --> 00:18:26.920
Forcing two things that are embarrassed to stand side by side to remain in the same room.

177
00:18:26.920 --> 00:18:33.930
And perhaps hardest of all: refusing to accept the first arrangement that occurs to you, breaking it up and laying it out again.

178
00:18:33.930 --> 00:18:42.540
In a letter he wrote to Hooke in sixteen seventy-five, Newton said that if he had been able to see further it was because he stood on the shoulders of giants.

179
00:18:42.540 --> 00:18:48.110
That sentence is not a display of modesty but a set of instructions.

180
00:18:48.110 --> 00:18:52.880
Climbing onto the shoulders is work too; nobody carries you up there.

181
00:18:52.880 --> 00:18:57.170
And so the question we opened with no longer stands as a dilemma.

182
00:18:57.170 --> 00:19:00.020
Creativity is finding, yes.

183
00:19:00.020 --> 00:19:07.510
But what you find is not a voice coming from outside; it is a possibility offered to you by the field you have placed yourself inside.

184
00:19:07.510 --> 00:19:15.160
The more you learn, the more different areas you get mixed up in, the more your number of pieces capable of touching each other increases.

185
00:19:15.160 --> 00:19:18.810
Inspiration goes to the house of the one who invites it.

186
00:19:18.810 --> 00:19:23.740
And there is a conclusion here about everyday life that is broader than we might suppose.

187
00:19:23.740 --> 00:19:27.230
You did not choose the material of your life either.

188
00:19:27.230 --> 00:19:36.960
The family you were born into, the first language you spoke, the city you grew up in, the century you fell into, your body, the fears you were taught.

189
00:19:36.960 --> 00:19:41.570
None of these are ordered parts; they were all already sitting in the refrigerator.

190
00:19:41.570 --> 00:19:46.020
The possibility of making a life from zero has been given to nobody.

191
00:19:46.020 --> 00:19:50.630
But you have far more say over the arrangement of those pieces than you think.

192
00:19:50.630 --> 00:19:55.400
With the same ingredients, two people build two completely different things.

193
00:19:55.400 --> 00:20:00.650
Creativity, then, is not a secret resource carried by a privileged few.

194
00:20:00.650 --> 00:20:03.500
It is a way of looking at what you have.

195
00:20:03.500 --> 00:20:14.190
Seeing the parsley set aside in the corner, thinking that there might be a link between two unrelated things, the habit of trying a second time, differently, what has already been tried once.

196
00:20:14.190 --> 00:20:18.480
And the loveliest thing about that habit is this: it can be taught.

197
00:20:18.480 --> 00:20:24.370
It is learned from a master, it settles in through repetition, it deepens as it is used.

198
00:20:24.370 --> 00:20:27.860
Making something out of nothing was not given to us.

199
00:20:27.860 --> 00:20:33.270
But the arrangement was left to us, and all human work lies inside that arrangement.

200
00:20:33.270 --> 00:20:37.240
Nöron Philosophy'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

201
00:20:37.240 --> 00:20:40.890
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
